title: Blinking LEDs
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-01-01.png
  - .assets/img-101-01-02.png

steps:

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

        {[ht,$start]%[cu]BlinkerP% &ndash; Blinking LEDs}

        Having completed the **Orientation** tours, you've already viewed (and executed) the {[cu]BlinkerP} program.

        In fact, you also know quite about the other **Units** used by this program &ndash; {[cu]BoardC}, {[cu]Common}, {[cu]AppLed}.

        {[in]We will highlight some other <b>EMS</b> features seen here.}

        To prepare for other **Basic Examples** tours, we'll now pivot to *real-time analysis* [RTA] of **EMS** programs.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 A 4
      - mark 1 B 7
      - mark 1 C 9
      - mark 1 D 11
      - mark 1 E 12
    focus: [1,1]
    acts: ['$build']
    text: |

        {[ht,$details]%[cu]BlinkerP% &ndash; A Few Details}

        More language features you've noticed along the way:

        <div class="em-bmlist">

        {[bm,A]} The {[ck]import} of {[cu]BoardC} uses {[cs]'@$distro'} to logically name the **Bucket** associated with the current &emsp;&nbsp; <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>.

        {[bm,B]} Like {[ck]import}, {[cs]$delegate} brings a **Unit** into scope &ndash; but without knowing its `'@bucket/Unit.em'` name.

        {[bm,C]} The {[cs]em$run} intrinsic provides a &laquo;`main`&raquo; program entry-point &ndash; executed after any {[cs]em$startup} calls.

        {[bm,D]} Local identifiers starting with `_` conventionally im&shy;ply their *non-usage* &ndash; silencing some **TS** warnings.

        {[bm,E]} Numeric constants can use `_` for readability &ndash; part of standard **TS** automatically inherited by **EMS**.

        </div>

  - cmds:
      - clear 1
      - view 2
    text: |

        {[ht,browse_activity]&thinsp;Introducing PulseView}

        The **Basic Examples** tours will leverage **PulseView** for real-time analysis [RTA] of **EMS** programs.

        {[in]The <b>EM&bull;Home</b> editor tab contains a link for installing this application on your PC.}

        <div class="em-bmlist">

        {[bm,1]} When running, the **Wokwi LOGIC** block wired to our simulated **Pi Pico** board captures signals **D0** - **D7**.

        {[bm,2]} Terminating the simulator with its **Stop ■** button saves a special `wokwi.vcd` file into your workspace.

        {[bm,3]} Reloading `wokwi.vcd` inside a running **PulseView** window will refresh the application's display.

        </div>

        {[in]To zoom the image in&middot;out, press <b>Ctrl</b> (or <b>Cmd</b>) within its editor tab while scrolling your mouse down&middot;up.}

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |

        {[ht,checklist]&thinsp;PulseView Setup}

        You should first **build&middot;load** {[cu]BlinkerP} and then explictly terminate the **Wokwi** Simulator using its **Stop ■** button.

        {[in]Status messages will pop-up in the lower-right corner of your <b>VS Code</b> window.&thinsp; Go <b>Back</b> for reference.}

        You'll now import `$HOME/EM/repo/emporium/wokwi.vcd` after launching the **PulseView** application.

        {[bm,A]} select **Import Value Change ...** then locate this file<br>
        {[bm,B]} set **Downsampling** to **125** before clicking **Ok**<br>
        {[bm,C]} drag the **UART** signal above the **D0** signal

        Feel free to keep **PulseView** active while working with other programs on these tours.





 



#   - cmds:
#       - build 1 -l
#     focus: [1,1]
#     acts: ['$build']
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$details]⟪[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP⟫ &ndash; build + load}
# 
#         Once {[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP} begins execution, you'' see {[cx]appLed} blink on&thinsp;/&thinsp;off five times before the program terminates.
#         
#         The EM runtime will turn-on the board's {[cx]sysLed} pin (typically a red LED) after the program terminates.
# 
#         You can re-run {[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP} by simply pressing the board's {[cx]RESET} button.
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - mark 1 A 5
#       - mark 1 B 7
#       - mark 1 C 9
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$details]⟪[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP⟫ &ndash; details}
# 
#         <div class="em-bmlist">
# 
#         {[bm,A]} The {[cu]Common} module exposes several proxies imple&shy;menting core MCU services, including {[cu]BusyWait}.
# 
#         {[bm,B]} The {[cu]BoardC} composite configures and exports the {[cu]AppLed} module, imported here by {[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP}
# 
#         {[bm,C]} The {[cf]em$run} intrinsic then invokes public functions of these two imported units.
# 
#         </div>
# 
#         {[cu]AppLed} and {[cu]Common}.{[cu]BusyWait} respectively implement the {[cu]AppLedI} and {[cu]BusyWaitI} interfaces, which we'll visit next.
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - open 2
#     focus: [2,1]
#     acts: ['$reveal']
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$peek]⟪[cu]LedI⟫ &ndash; quick peek}
# 
#         The {[cn]em.hal}/{[cu]LedI} interface specifies a set of functions which an LED must implement in the abstract.
# 
#         Our {[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP} program invoked several of these func&shy;tions within its {[cf]em$run} intrinsic.
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - open 3
#       - mark 3 A 4
#       - mark 3 B 12
#     focus: [3,1]
#     acts: ['$reveal']
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$details]⟪[cu]Common⟫ &ndash; details}
# 
#         <div class="em-bmlist">
# 
#         {[bm,A]} {[cu]Common} imports a number of abstract interfaces from the {[cn]em.hal} package, including {[cu]BusyWaitI}.
# 
#         {[bm,B]} The current {[cn]em$distro} package binds the {[cu]BusyWait} proxy and others at program build-time.
# 
#         </div>
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - open 4
#     focus: [4,1]
#     acts: ['$reveal']
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$peek]⟪[cu]BusyWaitI⟫ &ndash; quick peek}
# 
#         And last, {[cu]BusyWaitI} specifices a {[cf]wait} function &ndash; called from our {[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP} program to spin for 0.5 seconds.
# 
#         In practice, however, you'll want to use EM platform timers for delays longer than a few milliseconds
# 
#   - cmds: []
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$extra]⟪[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP⟫ &ndash; extra credit}
# 
#         {[bi,task_alt]} Study the logic capture(s) found {[le,https://docs.openem.org/using/using-02/#tour-01-basic-blinker]here}.
# 
#         {[bi,task_alt]} Clone {[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP} into your personal bundle.
# 
#         {[bi,task_alt]} Modify the value passed to {[cf]wait}.
# 
#         {[bi,sentiment_satisfied]} Happy coding&thinsp;!!!
# 
# 
# 
#   - cmds: []
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$steps]⟪[cu]Ex02_BlinkerP⟫ &ndash; next steps}
# 
#         The next several tours in the {[le,https://docs.openem.org/using/using-02]Using EM} series will build upon the code seen here.
# 
#         While introducing threads&thinsp;/&thinsp;events&thinsp;/&thinsp;timers in EM, these programs will otherwise just blink the LEDs.
# 
#         But first, let's add some "real-time" debug statements in **Tour&thinsp;02**. 
